Autoimmune & Inflammatory Neuropathic Syndromes
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Symmetrical ascending weakness, Areflexia, Cytoalbuminologic dissociation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-002
Reason for Visit: Rapid onset of bilateral lower extremity weakness and difficulty standing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute progression of lower limb weakness over 24 hours with new onset subjective breathlessness. Immediate assessment is required to evaluate respiratory muscle function and assess for impending neuromuscular respiratory failure before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs feel like heavy lead and I keep stumbling. The tingling in my toes is burning now."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a rapid onset of widespread distal sensory and motor deficits. Symptoms began 3 days ago as a glove-and-stocking distribution of paresthesias, numbness, and severe neuropathic pain. Over the last 24 hours, the patient developed symmetrical, ascending flaccid weakness initiating in the lower extremities, severely limiting ambulation. The patient notes mild lightheadedness upon standing, suggesting autonomic dysfunction presenting as orthostatic hypotension. No current facial paresis, dysphagia, or diplopia, but the severe allodynia specifically highlights small fiber pathology. A recent mild gastrointestinal illness resolved two weeks prior.
Functional Status: Bedbound due to severe lower extremity weakness and ataxia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone, unable to access toilet safely without high risk of falls.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 mmHg (lying), 85/55 mmHg (standing) | HR: 110 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 22 bpm | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 80 Kg
General: Alert and oriented, visibly anxious, lying supine in bed.
Physical Exam: Symmetrical ascending flaccid weakness in bilateral lower extremities (power 3/5 proximally, 2/5 distally). Diminished or completely absent deep tendon reflexes occurring early in the clinical course. Sensation to pinprick and light touch reduced in a stocking distribution up to the mid-calf. Cranial nerves intact. Chest is clear but respiratory effort is mildly increased. Areflexia confirmed. Diagnostic parameters correlate with expected cytoalbuminologic dissociation in cerebrospinal fluid.
Environmental Observations: House is cluttered, tripping hazards present, telephone out of reach from bed.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Nil known drug allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Autoimmune Neuropathic Syndrome (Suspected Guillain-Barre Syndrome) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Spinal Cord Compression - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of hyperreflexia, upgoing plantars, and no discrete sensory level on examination.

Myasthenia Gravis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent sensory symptoms, absent reflexes, and lack of prominent extraocular/bulbar weakness.

Hypokalemic Periodic Paralysis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of significant sensory deficits and neuropathic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits rapid progression of ascending flaccid weakness, areflexia, and autonomic dysfunction. The recent onset of tachycardia and mild tachypnea strongly raises the suspicion of impending diaphragmatic compromise. Postponing clinical evaluation until the following day carries a catastrophic risk of unobserved acute respiratory failure or severe falls due to orthostatic hypotension. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandatory to physically map the ascending neurological deficit, assess vital capacity at the bedside, and expedite immediate hospital transfer to secure the airway and initiate definitive immunomodulatory therapy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate coordination with ambulance services for transfer to Emergency Department for neurological consultation, vital capacity monitoring, and likely IVIG or plasmapheresis. Intravenous access secured on site.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By remaining strictly in bed until paramedics arrive, the patient prevents severe fall injuries caused by orthostatic hypotension and leg weakness.
Step 2: By taking slow, measured breaths when feeling anxious, the patient actively reduces metabolic demand and preserves respiratory muscle function.
Step 3: By allowing paramedics to lift and transfer using a slide sheet, the patient ensures a pain-free transition out of the home, reinforcing trust in medical care.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Hospital admission required.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Inpatient team to manage; GP review upon hospital discharge.

